  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy but dry today with pleasant temperatures\nahead of a cold front.\n\nA deepening low continues to track east across the James Bay \nand into Quebec today. Despite the cold front pushing through \nNew England later this afternoon, clearing skies and full sun \nwill help raise temperatures are expected into the upper 60s \ntoday. This front will also bring breezy conditions, with wind \ngusts on the order of 20 to 30 mph, especially over the waters \nand higher elevation regions, through tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying trend and fall-like conditions return \nfor the rest of the week.\n\nThe drying trend continues through the rest of the week as a \nstrong high pressures system builds in Tuesday over the Great \nLakes. This high brings a significantly cooler airmass into New \nEngland, with the latest guidance indicating the 540mb line \nsinking well into northern New England, likely as far south as \nsouthern NH, by late tonight or early Tuesday morning. 850mb \ntemperatures drop to near 0C, likely below 0C in the higher \nelevations. Most guidance also suggests 925mb temps drop to as \nlow as +1C or +2C. This would support surface highs near 60 \ndegrees with nighttime lows possibly falling into the 30s \nthrough Wednesday. Tuesday night looks to be the coldest of the \nseason due to radiational cooling from clear skies and low \nwinds.\n\nA brief warm-up to the low 70s may be possible late week as the\nhigh pushes east Thursday. Otherwise, prolonged west-southwest \nflow will mark the return of drier conditions and seasonable \ntemperatures to southern New England for the end of the week. \nSeeing some potential in the global models for some showers \ntoward the end of the weekend, but given the timeframe, it'll \nremain something to keep an eye on.",
